// `??` whose LEFT OPERAND is an if-EXPRESSION written IN PLACE. `vl check` returns 0 (the
// `if` is `i32 | null` and `??` over it is `i32`) and the emitter refuses with
// `emitProgram: \`??\` over this nullable value is not supported yet — narrow it first, e.g.
// \`if v != null { … }\`` (`compiler/wasmEmit.vl`, the fall-through past the value-union-box
// arm). Should print 7 then -1.
//
// THE MISSING `else` IS NOT AN INGREDIENT — ablated. `(if c { 7 } else { null }) ?? -1`
// refuses with the identical message, and so does a `string` arm and a call-argument
// position; only the spelling through a BINDING (`const a = if c { 7 }` then `a ?? -1`)
// runs. What the arm needs and does not get is a NAME for the box it is coalescing: it
// reads `unionNameOfExpr` of the `??` NODE, whose own checked type is the coalesced scalar
// (`i32`), so the name comes back empty and the arm declines. The binding spelling never
// reaches it — `emit_rewrite` desugars a RE-READABLE left operand into an `if`, and a
// binding is re-readable while an if-expression is not.
//
// D1250. Filed by the D1086 build, which made this program `vl check`-clean for the first
// time: the ruling gives an else-less `if` a value in every position, and `??` is one of
// the positions it names.
function go(): void {
  print((if 1 == 1 { 7 }) ?? -1)
  print((if 1 == 2 { 7 }) ?? -1)
}
go()
